## Approval Boundaries

- Ordinary reversible implementation work can proceed autonomously when it is already scoped and automated checks pass.
- Human approval is required for destructive or irreversible data operations, authentication or authorization boundaries, security-sensitive changes, secret handling, paid external services, production deployment, destructive schema migrations, major scope expansion, and infrastructure with significant operational or financial consequences.
{% if governance == "managed" %}
- Managed A2 work needs human approval before start. Managed A1 work can proceed, but cannot be completed without human approval.
- `make task-approve` is for humans only. AI agents must not autonomously record A1 or A2 approval.
{% endif %}

## Project State Reconciliation

Before declaring a change ready, check whether durable planning/status
documentation exists and whether this change materially affects it. Depending
on the project, that documentation may include roadmap or backlog notes,
milestones, next-task statements, deferred decisions, or phase/status
documentation. Do not assume any single path such as a fixed roadmap file.

If such artifacts exist and are affected, reconcile them with the actual
repository state: remove stale claims, update durable documentation truthfully,
mark complete only work actually delivered, and never mechanically advance a
roadmap or backlog. If nothing needs to change, state that reconciliation was
checked and no update was required.
{% if governance == "managed" %}
This project also keeps managed planning/status artifacts (roadmap, milestones,
backlog, and the task lifecycle). Reconcile roadmap/milestone/task-level
planning with the post-change repository state and re-evaluate the next
meaningful task where appropriate, preferring the smallest evidence-producing
next slice. Search for stale wording such as "next task", "planned",
"deferred", "not yet implemented", "open decision", "prerequisite", "upcoming",
or "blocked". End agent reports with a concise `Project State Check` that
states what became complete, the next meaningful step, and why it follows from
the current repository state.

The task lifecycle, approval controls, `project/state.yaml`, and generated
boards remain authoritative for task status. Reconciliation complements that
machinery by keeping higher-level planning truthful; it never replaces managed
state or edits it directly.
{% else %}
Lightweight projects do not require task selection, next-task statements,
roadmap thinking, or a managed-style report footer. Use the brief,
architecture, workflow, quality gates, and ADRs as durable context and update
them only when implementation teaches something durable.
{% endif %}

## Workflow

{% if workflow_mode == "local" %}
Workflow mode is `local`: agents may work in the current local repository.
Commits, pushes, and pull requests are optional unless requested.
{% elif workflow_mode == "branch" %}
Workflow mode is `branch`: agents should work on a dedicated branch. Commits
may be created, but pushing and pull requests are optional unless requested.
{% else %}
Workflow mode is `pr`: agents must work on a non-`main` branch, commit their own
changes, push to `origin`, and open a ready pull request. Do not push directly
to `main`.
{% endif %}

General reusable skills are in `.agents/skills/`. Codex adapter notes are in
`.codex/`. Capability skills, when selected by this profile, are under
`.agents/capabilities/skills/`.

{% if governance == "managed" %}
{% if workflow_mode != "local" %}
Before editing files, agents must be on a non-`main` branch. If the current
branch is `main`, create a dedicated branch for the task first.
{% endif %}

Managed lifecycle skills are in `.agents/managed/skills/`.
Project-specific context is in `project/` and `docs/`. Run
`make agent-pre-review TASK=<id>` before review, do not bypass controlled task
transitions, and keep dashboards synchronized and valid.
{% else %}
Project-specific context is in `project/brief.md`, `docs/`, and ADRs under
`docs/decisions/`. Keep changes small, run `make check`, and update the brief or
ADRs when implementation teaches something durable.
{% endif %}
